issues.problematic_code_snippet:
abstract class TauriActivity : WryActivity() {
  var pluginManager: PluginManager = PluginManager(this)
  override val handleBackNavigation: Boolean = true

findings.body:
In the new `setup` hook, `_app.manage(AppPlugin(*handle_ref))` dereferences `&PluginHandle<R>` and moves the value. `PluginHandle` derives only `Debug` and implements `Clone` manually (`crates/tauri/src/plugin.rs:124-131`). It is not `Copy`, so this is error E0507 ("cannot move out of a shared reference"). The `#[cfg(target_os = "android")]` gate means desktop builds pass and the break only appears in Android CI.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/app/plugin.rs" lines="135-143"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The extra `handle_ref` binding is unnecessary. Move the handle directly, or clone it if it is still needed afterward. Also note that nothing in the crate calls `state::<AppPlugin>()`, so the managed state is never read.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri/src/app/plugin.rs" lines="138-140"}
let handle = _api.register_android_plugin("app.tauri", "AppPlugin")?;
_app.manage(AppPlugin(handle));

`onBackButtonPress` calls `addPluginListener('app', 'back-button', ...)`, which invokes `plugin:app|register_listener`. That command only exists in the Kotlin `Plugin` base class, so it works on Android alone. The Rust app plugin's `invoke_handler` (`crates/tauri/src/app/plugin.rs:121-134`) does not register `register_listener`, and the `build.rs` change marks it as a default-enabled `core:app` permission on all platforms. On desktop or iOS, calling `onBackButtonPress` will reject with a command-not-found error, even though the JSDoc says it listens to Android back events.

::diff{file="packages/api/src/app.ts" lines="255-275"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Either guard the function (for example, reject or no-op when not on Android), or document that it is Android-only and make the failure explicit. Exporting the ACL entries for all platforms also widens the default surface for no desktop benefit.

`AppPlugin.exit` is annotated `@Command`, but it is not in the `core:app` command list in `build.rs`, so no permission is generated for it. The Tauri ACL denies undeclared commands, and no TS wrapper calls `plugin:app|exit`. The `exit` method is therefore dead code. It also means a JS `back-button` handler that wants to close the app has no supported way to do so, since the default exit path is skipped whenever a listener exists.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android/src/main/java/app/tauri/AppPlugin.kt" lines="48-53"}

::callout{type="info"}
If exit is intended, add it to `build.rs`, expose it in `packages/api/src/app.ts`, and regenerate the permission reference. If not, remove it.

When `hasListener('back-button')` is true, the callback only triggers the event and never calls `goBack()` or the activity default. This is a reasonable opt-in design, but the handler gets no fallback. A JS listener that just wants the default behavior has to re-implement it (for example, with `history.back()`), and the event payload has no way to request the native default. Combined with the unreachable `exit` command, an app that registers a listener and then decides to quit cannot close itself through the plugin.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android/src/main/java/app/tauri/AppPlugin.kt" lines="29-44"}

::callout{type="info"}
Consider documenting this behavior explicitly in `onBackButtonPress` JSDoc, or passing a flag so the handler can ask the native side to run the default action.

`TauriActivity` now sets `override val handleBackNavigation: Boolean = true`. This property comes from wry's `WryActivity`, which is not in this repo. If wry already registers its own back callback that calls `webView.goBack()` when `canGoBack`, then two callbacks run on every press: wry's, which navigates the WebView regardless of JS listeners, and `AppPlugin`'s, which should defer to the listener. In that case the `back-button` listener would be bypassed whenever the WebView can go back.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android-codegen/TauriActivity.kt" lines="12-16"}

::callout{type="warning"}
I could not confirm wry's default or its callback order from this repo. Please verify against the wry 0.53.4 `WryActivity` source. If the property is default-on, the override is a no-op. If it adds a competing callback, remove the override or make `AppPlugin` the single owner of back handling.

`Cargo.lock` includes changes that have nothing to do with the back-button feature. `wry` goes from 0.53.2 to 0.53.4 in both `Cargo.lock` and `crates/tauri-runtime-wry/Cargo.toml`, and several transitive crates move between `windows-sys` 0.48 and 0.59 and `windows-targets` 0.48 and 0.52. None of this is mentioned in the changelog entries. Bumping `windows-sys` across a major-version boundary can affect Windows builds and is hard to review in a lock-file diff.

::diff{file="Cargo.lock" lines="10946-10954"}

::callout{type="info"}
Split these into a separate dependency PR, or explain the reason in the description. If the wry bump is required for the Android back handling, say so.

`_app.manage(AppPlugin(...))` stores the handle in Tauri's state map, but the crate never calls `state::<AppPlugin<_>>()`. The `AppPlugin` struct is `pub(crate)` and only exists to hold the handle. Because the handle has no consumer, the state registration is dead code. The `_app` and `_api` underscore prefixes also suggest the arguments are unused, while they are in fact used on Android. That is confusing for a reader.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/app/plugin.rs" lines="147-148"}

::callout{type="info"}
If the handle is not needed after setup, drop the state and the struct. Otherwise, add a consumer and rename the parameters to `app` and `api`.

The PR adds native dispatch logic, a JS API, and ACL changes, but no tests or example changes cover them. The Kotlin callback branches (listener present, WebView can go back, default fallback) are untested, and there is no check that `onBackButtonPress` produces the expected `register_listener` invoke payload. The autogenerated `reference.md` was updated by hand, so it can drift from `build.rs` without a test catching it.

::diff{file="packages/api/src/app.ts" lines="255-275"}

::callout{type="info"}
At minimum, add a TS unit test that mocks `invoke` and checks the `register_listener` call and the handler wiring. Consider a permission-generation snapshot check.
